to <ei26 support> the republic Friday . \" This convention will be <ei28 seen> as a turning point in our history , \" Australian Republican Movement spokeswoman Mary Delahunty <ei29 said> . Pro-republicans <ei30 hope> to <ei31 have> an Australian president by the <ei32 opening> of the 2000 Olympics and the 100th anniversary of Australian federation . <ei34 Calling> for an amended constitution with a new preamble , to be <ei35 written> by Australian poets , republican Janet Holmes a Court <ei36 said> , \" We <ei37 need> the smell of eucalyptus in this , and the feel of red dust . \" Monarchists <ei38 hope> to <ei39 defeat> the republic at the <ei40 referendum> . \" The phony <ei41 war> has <ei42 finished> and the real referendum <ei43 campaign> has clearly <ei44 begun> , \" Australians for a Constitutional Monarchy executive director Kerry Jones <ei45 said> . \" Well may we <ei46 say> ` God save the Queen , ' for nothing will save the republic , \" outraged monarchist delegate David Mitchell <ei47 said> . \" The idea of a republic will <ei48 fall> to dust through the grace of almighty God . \" Polls have <ei49 shown> public support for a republic <ei50 increasing> , <ei51 rising> from about 35 percent several years ago to about 51 percent this year , as pro-monarchist sentiment <ei54 diminishes> . Australia has been <ei55 independent> since 1901 , but like many Commonwealth nations it still <ei57 recognizes> the British monarch as its head of state . Howard <ei58 said> Queen Elizabeth II is <ei59 taking> a great interest in the <ei60 convention> . \" She is , I can <ei61 assure> you , from what I 've been <ei62 told> , she 's <ei63 following> the thing very closely and <ei64 taking> the view it 's our business . \" It 's no secret that she 's <ei65 indicated> to me ... she is a 20th century democrat , <ei67 understands> that the role of the crown in Australia <ei68 rests> in the hands of the Australian people as it <ei69 has> for almost a hundred years . \" The model